Today I completed the design proposal for the WebCrawler project. I have not added much detail about the fetcher yet because I wanted to first understand how it works before finalizing its design. However, I completed the overall architecture and basic layout of the project.

After completing the proposal, I started building the project. I first created the basic project structure with separate include and src folders. Then I wrote the header file for the WebCrawler class, which will act as the blueprint for the crawler.

Initially, according to my design proposal, I planned to create a separate Frontier class that would simply wrap a LinkedList and behave like a FIFO queue. However, while implementing my Data Structures Library, I had already created a Queue data structure that internally uses a private LinkedList object and provides operations such as push(), pop(), size(), and empty(). Since it already fulfilled the requirements of the frontier, I decided to reuse it instead of creating another wrapper. While implementing the queue, I also learned about the Rule of Zero, which says that if a class contains members that manage their own resources, then the compiler-generated destructor, copy constructor, and copy assignment operator are usually sufficient.

Next, I started working on the fetcher. Since I did not know much about how a fetcher works, I spent some time learning about it. I learned that there are two types of web pages: static and dynamic. Static pages return all their content in the HTML itself, whereas dynamic pages load some content later using JavaScript on the client side. To keep the project simple initially, I decided that my fetcher will only support static HTML pages.

I chose to use libcurl for downloading web pages. I learned about the libcurl Easy Interface and how it performs synchronous network requests. I created a simple program that downloads the HTML of a webpage and stores it in a std::string. During this process, I learned how curl_easy_init() creates a handle, how curl_easy_setopt() is used to configure the request, and why CURLOPT_URL, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, and CURLOPT_WRITEDATA are important.

After understanding the basics, I implemented the Fetcher class by creating fetcher.h and fetcher.cpp. While testing it, I encountered a linker error because CMake was not linking the libcurl library. I fixed this by adding target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE CURL::libcurl). After that, I encountered another error because I had forgotten to call find_package(CURL REQUIRED) before linking the library. Once I added it, the project built successfully.

By the end of the day, I was able to successfully fetch the HTML of a webpage and store the complete response inside a string. This gives me a working foundation for the fetcher, and I can now move on to implementing the remaining components of the WebCrawler.